  "content": {
    "id": "p14-q10-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "10",
    "prompt": "The sum of two numbers is $6$. Find the maximum value of their product.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$9$, attained when both numbers are $3$.\n\nThe product is $9-(x-3)^2$, which cannot exceed $9$.",
      "worked": "Let the numbers be $x$ and $y$. Since $x+y=6$, $y=6-x$.\n$$\\begin{align*}xy&=x(6-x)\\\\xy&=-x^2+6x\\\\xy&=-(x^2-6x)\\\\xy&=-(x^2-6x+9)+9\\\\xy&=-(x-3)^2+9\\\\xy&\\leq9.\\end{align*}$$\nEquality holds when $x=3$ and $y=3$. The maximum product is $9$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 68
  },
